p=O/'physics-development.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');section='''<section id="force-bank-separation"><h2>Separating force direction from payload bank</h2><p>The acceleration consistency check permits an additional observable: the tilt λ of the net nongravitational force in the vertical plane normal to the inferred air-relative horizontal velocity. This is not a measurement of canopy roll. It includes propulsion and payload forces as well as canopy aerodynamics, and depends on the wind assumption.</p><pre>λ = atan2(f_specific · e_right, −f_specific,D)
δ = max(right_pull − right_offset, 0) − max(left_pull − left_offset, 0)
τ δ̇_filtered = δ − δ_filtered
λ̈ = Kδ (V/5)² (1.385/m) δ_filtered − Kλ sin λ − Dλ λ̇ + Bλ throttle
φ̈ = Kp sin(λ − φ) + Dp (λ̇ − φ̇) + Bp throttle</pre><p>This reduced diagnostic distinguishes force tilt λ from payload bank φ. It is not a derivation of all two-body forces: the throttle biases and fitted gains remain effective coefficients. Initial force tilt and rate use a quadratic fit to the preceding 0.4 seconds of accelerometer-derived tilt; no future force observations initialize the free prediction. Initial airspeed is held constant. Reference force traces use centered smoothing for scoring.</p><figure><img src="iteration-force-bank.png" alt="Measured and predicted force tilt with free and measured-force-conditioned bank replays"><figcaption>Top: the missing brake-to-force response. Bottom: payload-bank replay improves when supplied the measured force direction throughout the interval. The orange conditional curves use future measurements and are explicitly not simulator forecasts.</figcaption></figure><p>Across three chronological cross-validation blocks per flight, the free model produces 5.05 degrees RMS force-tilt error and 5.98 degrees RMS payload-bank error. Supplying measured force tilt to a separately fitted payload response reduces bank error to 2.68 degrees RMS. In the early Trout Lake 402-second turn, bank error falls from 10.55 to 1.97 degrees; in the late 3387-second turn it falls from 2.37 to 1.12 degrees. Hood River retains larger conditional errors (4–6 degrees in the three tight-turn development cases). This localizes much of the discrepancy upstream of payload bank, but does not prove the payload model complete.</p><p>The free force model's damping approaches its upper search bound. Its high-bank predictions remain inadequate, so this candidate is not accepted for controller design. Next identification should focus on how brake input, yaw/sideslip and changing speed redirect net force; changing rigging offsets alone has already failed to fix the held-out mismatch. Evidence: <code>force-bank-fit.json</code>, <code>payload-given-force.json</code>; reproducible code: <code>prepare_force_state.py</code>, <code>force_bank.cpp</code>, <code>fit_force_bank.py</code>, <code>fit_payload_given_force.py</code>.</p></section>''';s=s.replace('</html>',section+'</html>');p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')

